---
import DocumentLayout from '../layouts/DocumentLayout.astro';
import LeadStrand from '../components/LeadStrand.astro';
import Compare from '../components/Compare.astro';
import SurfaceLedger from '../components/SurfaceLedger.astro';
import Needs from '../components/Needs.astro';
import Draft from '../components/Draft.astro';

const R = '/work/review';

/**
 * Figures are countable from the source directories; feedback lines are verbatim
 * from FEEDBACK.md; the before/after strips are screenshots of the two builds
 * running side by side on localhost. Nothing here is reconstructed.
 */
const counts = [
  { n: '48', label: 'itemised notes' },
  { n: '6', label: 'surfaces reviewed' },
  { n: '394', label: 'lines changed next' },
  { n: '4', label: 'files touched' },
];
---

<DocumentLayout
  title="Forty-eight notes, and what happened to them"
  metaTitle="The review loop — feedback against a running build | Tyler Young"
  description="A stakeholder reviewed a running build and wrote forty-eight notes. Here is the before, the notes, and the after — including the half that was declined."
  descriptionDraft
  eyebrow="03 · How I Work"
  meta={[
    ['Artifact', 'One feedback round · 48 items · 6 surfaces'],
    ['Response', '4 files · 394 lines, in the next version'],
    ['Method', 'Review against a running build, not a mockup'],
    ['Shown', 'Only the regions that changed — client brand and staff excluded'],
  ]}
>
  <p class="answer">
    <Draft>
      A stakeholder sat with a running build and wrote forty-eight specific notes,
      organised by surface. The next version changed four files. Both builds still
      run, so you can watch the whole exchange — including the half that was turned
      down.
    </Draft>
  </p>

  <dl class="counts">
    {
      counts.map((c) => (
        <div>
          <dt>{c.n}</dt>
          <dd>{c.label}</dd>
        </div>
      ))
    }
  </dl>

  <h2>Three notes, and what they did</h2>
  <p>
    <Draft>
      Nobody writes “fix the colour coding — exceeding target versus falling behind
      should use different colours” about a picture of a dashboard. You write it
      after looking at a real one and finding you cannot tell good from bad at a
      glance.
    </Draft>
  </p>

  <Compare
    note="Progress visuals: Fix color coding — exceeding target vs falling behind should use different colors"
    before={`${R}/kpis-v5.png`}
    after={`${R}/kpis-v6.png`}
    beforeAlt="Four performance cards in a row, all with plain white backgrounds and identical amber value text, so a card 20 percent over target looks the same as one 7 percent short of it."
    afterAlt="The same four cards, each now carrying a coloured left edge with matching value text — orange, red, teal and amber — so over-target and short-of-target read differently at a glance."
    tell="Status moved from a uniform amber into four states, carried by a coloured edge as well as by the text."
  />

  <Compare
    note="Rename “Executive Intervention Queue” to “Intervention Queue”"
    before={`${R}/anchors-v5.png`}
    after={`${R}/anchors-v6.png`}
    beforeAlt="Anchor bar reading Key Performance Indicators and Intervention Que, with Queue misspelled."
    afterAlt="The same anchor bar, now reading Key Performance Indicators and Intervention Queue."
    tell="The rename also caught a spelling nobody had filed — “Que” had been sitting in the anchor bar the whole time."
  />

  <Compare
    note="Make intervention rows collapsible. Add filters (by status) and expand/collapse all icons"
    before={`${R}/queue-v5.png`}
    after={`${R}/queue-v6.png`}
    beforeAlt="Intervention section heading with a single Filter link floated to its right."
    afterAlt="Intervention Queue heading with a Filter button beneath it and a right-aligned toolbar of three controls for filtering, export and sorting."
    tell="One filter link became a control group. The collapsible rows in the same note did not land — see below."
  />

  <h2>The half that didn't happen</h2>
  <p>
    <Draft>
      Five component files were never touched. Every note covering the AI response
      panel, the drawer, the live session view and both navigation components is
      still sitting in the file, unactioned.
    </Draft>
  </p>

  <div class="ledger-slot"><SurfaceLedger /></div>

  <p>
    <Draft>
      That is not a failed round. A review produces a queue, not a checklist to be
      emptied, and someone decided which half was worth a version. What is unusual
      is that the decision is legible at all. Review a mockup and there is no diff
      to read — the prioritisation lives in somebody's memory. Review running code
      and it becomes a record you can audit later, which is exactly what I did to
      build this page.
    </Draft>
  </p>

  <h2>What happened after</h2>
  <p>
    <Needs>
      Tyler — whether the remaining items were picked up in a later round, and how
      many rounds this surface went through in total. Six directories is not six
      review rounds.
    </Needs>
  </p>

  <div class="aside">
    <p>
      Screens are cropped to the regions that changed. That is the honest framing
      for a before-and-after, and it keeps the client's brand, staff names and
      market references outside the frame. The full build is not published.
    </p>
  </div>

  <LeadStrand
    slot="after"
    items={[
      {
        slug: 'how-i-shaped-cross-functional-reputation',
        note: 'Putting a stakeholder in front of running code changes what the rest of the org thinks design is for. It is a reputational move before it is a technical one, and it only works if design has already earned the room.',
      },
      {
        slug: 'how-i-built-culture-around-paradox',
        note: 'Forty-eight notes and four changed files is a judgement about which half mattered. Knowing which bars are load-bearing and which are vanity is the whole discipline of shipping inside a fixed window.',
      },
    ]}
  />
</DocumentLayout>

<style>
  .answer {
    font-size: 24px !important;
    line-height: 1.52 !important;
    margin-bottom: 36px !important;
  }

  .counts {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: 22px 20px;
    margin: 0 0 40px;
    padding: 24px 0;
  }

  .counts div {
    display: grid;
    gap: 4px;
  }

  .counts dt {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: clamp(30px, 5vw, 40px);
    font-weight: 620;
    letter-spacing: -0.03em;
    line-height: 1;
  }

  .counts dd {
    margin: 0;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 10px;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--color-ink-3);
    line-height: 1.5;
  }

  .ledger-slot {
    margin: 0 0 34px;
  }

  @media (min-width: 810px) {
    .counts {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(4, minmax(0, 1fr));
    }
  }
</style>
